789
00:37:20.250 --> 00:37:20.570
Okay.

790
00:37:21.310 --> 00:37:24.130
Well, Colette and Eli, those are great questions.

791
00:37:24.450 --> 00:37:27.330
You know, I never thought Angela was from Scranton.

792
00:37:27.630 --> 00:37:32.790
I saw her sort of like my friends that I grew up with in Indonesia whose parents were in the

793
00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:38.090
military and they moved around a lot, which might also explain her need for structure

794
00:37:38.090 --> 00:37:39.950
and why she liked Dwight.

795
00:37:40.310 --> 00:37:44.230
And I also saw her as someone that needed order and rules.

796
00:37:44.230 --> 00:37:48.090
And I think that's why she picked accounting, you know, numbers make sense.

797
00:37:48.350 --> 00:37:48.530
Yeah.

798
00:37:49.650 --> 00:37:57.010
And as far as my actor prep, you know, I'm a people watcher and I kind of collect characters

799
00:37:57.010 --> 00:37:58.250
as I go through life.

800
00:37:58.590 --> 00:38:00.810
I'll jot down little notes about people I meet.

801
00:38:01.010 --> 00:38:04.310
Jenna, I'll leave you messages about people who I think are great characters.

802
00:38:04.510 --> 00:38:06.550
We kind of do that all the time with each other.

803
00:38:06.730 --> 00:38:10.890
You do it more though, Angela, in your descriptions of people you've observed.

804
00:38:10.890 --> 00:38:16.850
They're always so loving and so detailed and you find like just in the world, you find so

805
00:38:16.850 --> 00:38:17.590
many characters.

806
00:38:17.810 --> 00:38:18.830
This is so true about you.

807
00:38:18.930 --> 00:38:23.970
Well, I think people are so fascinating, you know, and I usually mesh a few of those

808
00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:26.050
different people when I start to create a role.

809
00:38:26.730 --> 00:38:33.350
So for Angela Martin, she was a combination of this woman who I worked with at 1-800-DENTISt.

810
00:38:33.590 --> 00:38:34.930
She was the office manager.

811
00:38:35.190 --> 00:38:37.790
She was in charge of front reception, the mailroom.

812
00:38:37.790 --> 00:38:39.950
And I've shared this before on the podcaSt.

813
00:38:40.270 --> 00:38:44.630
She was in charge of planning the Christmas party, the big company Christmas party.

814
00:38:45.070 --> 00:38:49.190
And she was allowed a few people to help her and you got a little extra money.

815
00:38:49.350 --> 00:38:51.010
And I signed up to do it.

816
00:38:51.170 --> 00:38:53.730
But man, was this party high stakes for her.

817
00:38:53.890 --> 00:38:55.330
She took it so seriously.

818
00:38:56.050 --> 00:39:00.410
And I've shared this before, but she literally yelled at me because I was doing a Christmas

819
00:39:00.410 --> 00:39:04.910
bow incorrectly on the stair banister leading into the party.

820
00:39:05.150 --> 00:39:06.410
She was like, let me do it.

821
00:39:06.410 --> 00:39:07.310
I was like, okay.

822
00:39:07.810 --> 00:39:09.970
So I had that lady in mind.

823
00:39:10.010 --> 00:39:13.370
And then the second person I used for Angela Martin was my grandmother.

824
00:39:13.910 --> 00:39:19.010
Now, she wasn't judgy or mean, but she was very, very proper.

825
00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:21.470
She was just a very proper lady.

826
00:39:21.730 --> 00:39:23.770
She always dressed up for the day.

827
00:39:23.990 --> 00:39:27.750
She wore lots of brooches and she had a lot of cats.

828
00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:33.210
She had this barn and she had all these barn cats that she would feed.

829
00:39:33.710 --> 00:39:37.550
I mean, I remember one time going over there and I looked out the back door because it was

830
00:39:37.550 --> 00:39:40.510
getting near dinner time and there were so many cats.

831
00:39:40.970 --> 00:39:47.870
And part of my family lore story is that as her eyesight grew worse, as she aged, one time

832
00:39:47.870 --> 00:39:50.350
a bobcat came up to the back door.

833
00:39:50.490 --> 00:39:50.830
Oh my gosh.

834
00:39:51.010 --> 00:39:51.830
And she fed it?

835
00:39:51.830 --> 00:39:52.550
And she fed the bobcat.

836
00:39:52.830 --> 00:39:53.110
No.

837
00:39:53.650 --> 00:39:53.970
Yes.

838
00:39:54.270 --> 00:39:56.330
And she was like, oh, I've got the biggest cat.

839
00:39:56.370 --> 00:39:58.170
And we were all there one time and it locked up.

840
00:39:58.230 --> 00:40:00.750
My dad was like, mom, that's a bobcat.

841
00:40:02.150 --> 00:40:05.150
So that is also part of Angela Martin for me.

842
00:40:05.330 --> 00:40:08.290
Now, is that why you had a picture of her on your desk?

843
00:40:08.610 --> 00:40:09.010
Yes.

844
00:40:09.430 --> 00:40:09.830
Yes.

845
00:40:09.830 --> 00:40:11.570
I had a picture of her and I.

846
00:40:11.830 --> 00:40:13.290
It was a black and white photo.

847
00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:15.390
It was in a frame that said, I heart grandma.

848
00:40:15.910 --> 00:40:18.130
And my eyes are closed in the picture.

849
00:40:18.270 --> 00:40:18.890
It's so wonderful.

850
00:40:19.210 --> 00:40:19.970
But she would do that.

851
00:40:20.150 --> 00:40:24.190
She would frame pictures of you and there would be someone's finger over the lens,

852
00:40:24.290 --> 00:40:24.990
but it went in a frame.

853
00:40:25.250 --> 00:40:25.490
Yeah.

854
00:40:25.590 --> 00:40:25.810
You know?

855
00:40:26.050 --> 00:40:26.210
Yeah.

856
00:40:26.450 --> 00:40:28.050
It didn't matter if your eyes were open or closed.

857
00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:30.390
She was going to put it in a frame and display it.

858
00:40:30.790 --> 00:40:35.330
Another thing I drew on as I shaped Angela Martin was actually, and I've never shared this

859
00:40:35.330 --> 00:40:35.630
before.

860
00:40:35.790 --> 00:40:37.150
I have never told you this, Jenna.

861
00:40:37.490 --> 00:40:37.790
What?

862
00:40:38.370 --> 00:40:42.690
So when I was doing all my auditions, you know, I auditioned first for Pam.

863
00:40:42.970 --> 00:40:43.090
Yeah.

864
00:40:43.350 --> 00:40:48.210
And then between that time and when I came in for Angela Martin was several months.

865
00:40:48.650 --> 00:40:53.390
And I had started reading the book, The Age of Innocence by Edith Wharton.

866
00:40:53.690 --> 00:40:53.950
Okay.

867
00:40:53.950 --> 00:41:00.590
And I look back now on that and I think about how informing that book was for me as I went

868
00:41:00.590 --> 00:41:00.770
in.

869
00:41:00.890 --> 00:41:03.890
I didn't know, you know, I didn't know I was going to audition for Angela Martin.

870
00:41:04.150 --> 00:41:06.010
It just sort of happened.

871
00:41:06.610 --> 00:41:12.370
And I was immersed in this world of like 1870s New York society.

872
00:41:12.670 --> 00:41:13.670
It was the golden age.

873
00:41:13.810 --> 00:41:15.390
There were all these social rules.

874
00:41:15.630 --> 00:41:19.070
There was repressed feelings and unconsummated love.

875
00:41:19.070 --> 00:41:24.590
And I just feel like Angela is someone that would have felt right at home in an older,

876
00:41:24.750 --> 00:41:26.070
more conservative world.

877
00:41:26.230 --> 00:41:26.850
For sure.

878
00:41:27.130 --> 00:41:30.630
And that's what I was reading when I went into audition.

879
00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:32.810
And I'd sort of forgotten about that.

880
00:41:32.910 --> 00:41:37.210
And then I went back to my journal that I was keeping that year.

881
00:41:38.090 --> 00:41:42.270
And I wrote in my journal about this book and how I kept thinking about it.

882
00:41:42.330 --> 00:41:45.950
And I kept thinking about these characters and how the ending broke my heart.

883
00:41:46.110 --> 00:41:49.270
And that's what I was reading when I went in for my audition.

884
00:41:49.550 --> 00:41:52.530
I am getting chills at the serendipity of that.

885
00:41:52.870 --> 00:41:58.030
How there are these times in an artist's life where there are things that are informing

886
00:41:58.030 --> 00:42:02.230
your art and your career and you don't even know it.

887
00:42:02.370 --> 00:42:02.570
Yeah.

888
00:42:02.570 --> 00:42:06.470
You don't even know like why you're doing it or how it's going to happen.

889
00:42:06.690 --> 00:42:12.750
It's this like, it's part of the weird magic of our industry and what we do that, oh my

890
00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:14.330
God, I love that story so much.

891
00:42:14.530 --> 00:42:14.830
Yeah.

892
00:42:14.830 --> 00:42:18.950
I was sort of inviting this creative narrative.

893
00:42:18.950 --> 00:42:20.750
I didn't even realize I was inviting.

894
00:42:20.910 --> 00:42:25.530
And when I went in to read for the role of, at the time, just the persnickety accountant,

895
00:42:25.910 --> 00:42:26.250
right?

896
00:42:26.790 --> 00:42:32.230
I made this choice to have really, really good, proper posture.

897
00:42:32.290 --> 00:42:37.610
And I sat shoulders back, like straight up and almost to the point where like, I like

898
00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:39.770
got a back ache after.

899
00:42:39.870 --> 00:42:39.990
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

900
00:42:40.090 --> 00:42:41.650
Because you were really forcing this posture.

901
00:42:41.790 --> 00:42:42.030
Yeah.

902
00:42:42.210 --> 00:42:44.270
That's how I went in the room with this posture.

903
00:42:44.270 --> 00:42:46.990
And I think that book sort of was informing me.

904
00:42:47.090 --> 00:42:52.790
So I sort of feel like I went in there with this sort of like persona I didn't even realize.

905
00:42:52.790 --> 00:42:53.490
Mm-hmm.

906
00:42:54.010 --> 00:42:54.390
Yeah.

907
00:42:54.790 --> 00:42:56.390
I just love that so much.

