00:45:50.350 --> 00:45:54.110
We've talked so much on the show, Ang, about how different you are from your character.

984
00:45:54.310 --> 00:45:57.330
I mean, anyone who listens to the podcast can hear that.

985
00:45:57.510 --> 00:46:01.950
And we had a lot of people write in about the differences between you and your character.

986
00:46:02.550 --> 00:46:04.350
For example, Amanda E.

987
00:46:04.470 --> 00:46:05.450
from Illinois said,

988
00:46:05.570 --> 00:46:10.870
I'm so excited for this episode because I think Angela is one of the most underrated characters in the show.

989
00:46:11.390 --> 00:46:15.410
A question I'm curious about, and I'm sure many people will ask is,

990
00:46:15.730 --> 00:46:21.030
how did it feel to play a character that has a demeanor so opposite of your natural self?

991
00:46:21.030 --> 00:46:26.050
Was it difficult to play a cold and stony character for almost a decade?

992
00:46:27.250 --> 00:46:31.250
Oh, well, Amanda, that's really nice of you to say all of that.

993
00:46:31.610 --> 00:46:35.090
You know, Angela Martin was just an escape for me.

994
00:46:35.290 --> 00:46:37.830
In real life, I am not a very confrontational person.

995
00:46:38.130 --> 00:46:39.650
I don't like to make a fuss.

996
00:46:39.650 --> 00:46:43.770
So getting to play someone who's not afraid to just let people have it,

997
00:46:44.670 --> 00:46:46.390
it was actually really fun.

998
00:46:46.670 --> 00:46:49.770
I do have to say, I had the hardest time being mean to Phyllis.

999
00:46:50.110 --> 00:46:52.770
Phyllis is literally the nicest person on the earth.

1000
00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:57.210
I know, and she just has the sweetest face, and it was so hard to be mean to her.

1001
00:46:57.590 --> 00:47:02.730
And I did struggle with how judgy Angela could be, and I also struggled with all the buns.

1002
00:47:03.170 --> 00:47:04.370
Oh, literally, headaches.

1003
00:47:04.570 --> 00:47:06.930
Headaches, like the pulled hair and the bobby pins.

1004
00:47:06.930 --> 00:47:10.790
I don't know how people wear such high, tight buns for hours and hours.

1005
00:47:11.490 --> 00:47:16.270
And, you know, my friends did joke that, like, if I would go and meet them out after work,

1006
00:47:16.430 --> 00:47:19.310
after a day of filming, like, you know, go do improv or whatever,

1007
00:47:19.530 --> 00:47:21.850
they said I would have resting bitch face.

1008
00:47:22.750 --> 00:47:26.410
That I had spent all day not allowing myself to smile.

1009
00:47:26.810 --> 00:47:30.390
And people, I've had strangers, like, stop me and be like, hey, you okay?

1010
00:47:30.610 --> 00:47:31.090
And I'm like, what?

1011
00:47:31.250 --> 00:47:32.130
No, I'm great.

1012
00:47:32.350 --> 00:47:36.370
But I guess I just have this, like, scowly frown on my face.

1013
00:47:36.370 --> 00:47:39.830
Did you feel like that finally went away after the show ended?

1014
00:47:40.090 --> 00:47:41.530
Do you still get that from people?

1015
00:47:41.750 --> 00:47:42.290
I feel like...

1016
00:47:42.290 --> 00:47:43.390
No, not as much anymore.

1017
00:47:43.390 --> 00:47:45.650
I think my face is finally relaxed.

1018
00:47:46.750 --> 00:47:48.230
It took years, though.

1019
00:47:49.110 --> 00:47:53.230
Well, I have a question, sort of a follow-up, which is,

1020
00:47:53.370 --> 00:47:58.050
how has playing the role of Angela Martin affected you in real life?

1021
00:47:58.090 --> 00:48:00.170
I mean, I know you said people would stop you on the street.

1022
00:48:00.170 --> 00:48:05.410
Or I guess another way to say it is, like, how has it affected your career,

1023
00:48:05.770 --> 00:48:11.710
being so known for a type of person that is, like, so different from who you are?

1024
00:48:12.170 --> 00:48:14.590
And we also got a fan letter from Kelly W.

1025
00:48:14.930 --> 00:48:17.670
in West Africa slash Oregon slash Ontario.

1026
00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:18.270
Kelly!

1027
00:48:18.630 --> 00:48:19.750
You get around, Kelly.

1028
00:48:19.750 --> 00:48:24.590
Who said, Angela Martin has quickly become my absolute favorite character.

1029
00:48:24.790 --> 00:48:30.890
She always has the best reactions and also fully embodies the concept of schnadenfreude.

1030
00:48:31.170 --> 00:48:31.890
Look it up.

1031
00:48:32.170 --> 00:48:33.050
Schnadenfreude, okay.

1032
00:48:33.330 --> 00:48:37.730
Knowing Angela's true personality now makes it an even more outstanding performance.

1033
00:48:38.150 --> 00:48:43.190
My question is, was Angela being typecast before The Office?

1034
00:48:43.210 --> 00:48:45.070
Or did it begin afterwards?

1035
00:48:45.070 --> 00:48:48.030
And have you had to work hard to get different roles?

1036
00:48:48.590 --> 00:48:49.510
Oh, gosh.

1037
00:48:49.670 --> 00:48:51.370
It's a lot of questions I just threw at you.

1038
00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:51.650
It is.

1039
00:48:51.750 --> 00:48:53.290
Well, you know, no.

1040
00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:56.930
Angela was the first B-I-T-C-H I ever played.

1041
00:48:57.110 --> 00:48:57.330
Okay.

1042
00:48:57.630 --> 00:48:57.790
Yeah.

1043
00:48:58.170 --> 00:49:03.130
And Greg did say to me, you know, there's good career safety because in an office,

1044
00:49:03.130 --> 00:49:04.370
there's always one bitch.

1045
00:49:04.930 --> 00:49:07.730
So he said, that's actually job security.

1046
00:49:07.990 --> 00:49:12.250
Yes, for future office-related projects.

1047
00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:12.930
That's right.

1048
00:49:13.370 --> 00:49:16.210
But, you know, after The Office, I did.

1049
00:49:16.350 --> 00:49:21.170
I got a series of roles where people wanted me to play the persnickety lady.

1050
00:49:21.510 --> 00:49:21.710
Yeah.

1051
00:49:21.990 --> 00:49:23.770
I had a few jobs right after The Office.

1052
00:49:24.090 --> 00:49:26.950
One was on New Girl, and I did not play the bitch on that one.

1053
00:49:27.010 --> 00:49:27.890
So that was fun.

1054
00:49:28.270 --> 00:49:31.850
But I did get a series of offers to play the bitch.

1055
00:49:31.990 --> 00:49:35.230
And I only took one because it was with Betty White.

1056
00:49:35.470 --> 00:49:35.930
Mm-hmm.

1057
00:49:36.110 --> 00:49:37.190
It was hot in Cleveland.

1058
00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:38.230
That's a good one.

1059
00:49:38.230 --> 00:49:42.890
And I was like, I have, I mean, she's just this legend, and I just want to occupy the

1060
00:49:42.890 --> 00:49:44.430
same space with her just once.

1061
00:49:44.730 --> 00:49:49.630
And I was trying not to just go back to that well of like, oh, people are wanting to cast

1062
00:49:49.630 --> 00:49:50.850
me as the persnickety lady.

1063
00:49:51.230 --> 00:49:53.650
But I remember telling my agents, yes, I will do it.

1064
00:49:53.730 --> 00:49:54.850
I will absolutely do it.

1065
00:49:54.850 --> 00:49:58.510
And I found an interview where I was asked about it.

1066
00:49:58.630 --> 00:50:01.530
I was on the set of Hot in Cleveland, and here's what I had to say.

1067
00:50:04.880 --> 00:50:09.200
Hello, my name is Angela Kinsey, and I will be playing Dr. Teresa Carson.

1068
00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:12.520
I am the headmistress of a snooty preschool.

1069
00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:13.500
And I'm Julie.

1070
00:50:14.540 --> 00:50:16.960
Wow, that is a big hand.

1071
00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:22.600
She reminds me a little bit of my office character and the fact that she's, well, she takes everything

1072
00:50:22.600 --> 00:50:23.420
very seriously.

1073
00:50:23.940 --> 00:50:25.720
But she wears a lot better suits.

1074
00:50:25.720 --> 00:50:29.000
You know, I met Betty White, and she held my hand when we were talking.

1075
00:50:29.060 --> 00:50:31.200
And I was like, she told me what she is.

1076
00:50:31.620 --> 00:50:32.760
She's like so cute.

1077
00:50:32.880 --> 00:50:34.060
I didn't want to hug her.

1078
00:50:34.400 --> 00:50:38.520
And I don't know why everyone's so tall, minus Betty.

1079
00:50:39.140 --> 00:50:43.240
Of course, Betty's taller than me because, you know, I'm 5'1".

1080
00:50:43.240 --> 00:50:45.920
But no, it's like the land of tall people.

1081
00:50:46.520 --> 00:50:50.140
This week, we also have guest starring Craig Ferguson, who I love.

1082
00:50:50.640 --> 00:50:52.100
He's already cracking me up.

1083
00:50:52.100 --> 00:50:56.880
So I'm a little worried about Tape Knight because he looks at me with the goofiest look

1084
00:50:56.880 --> 00:50:59.620
on his face so many times in the scene, and I just lose it.

1085
00:51:00.520 --> 00:51:06.660
So yeah, I realized I was playing another version of Angela Martin on that show, but I just was

1086
00:51:06.660 --> 00:51:07.880
so delighted to be there.

1087
00:51:07.880 --> 00:51:13.680
But it is one reason why I was so thrilled to be in Haters Back Off on Netflix and the

1088
00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:20.220
tall girl movies because I got to play sort of just like these moms that were very interesting

1089
00:51:20.220 --> 00:51:22.760
like characters, but they weren't bitchy.

1090
00:51:22.900 --> 00:51:24.520
And then your Hallmark Christmas movie.

1091
00:51:24.640 --> 00:51:25.320
Oh my gosh.

1092
00:51:25.600 --> 00:51:28.420
Is maybe the closest thing to you in real life.

1093
00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:29.160
Probably.

1094
00:51:29.480 --> 00:51:32.300
Just like you love a holiday.

1095
00:51:32.880 --> 00:51:34.100
I do love a holiday.

1096
00:51:34.280 --> 00:51:35.100
You're a good neighbor.

1097
00:51:35.100 --> 00:51:37.980
I'm not going to make everyone wear a onesie all day, though.

1098
00:51:38.260 --> 00:51:39.080
That's true.

1099
00:51:39.080 --> 00:51:42.320
You love a holiday, but you don't force people to dress up or anything.

1100
00:51:42.620 --> 00:51:42.880
That's true.

1101
00:51:43.440 --> 00:51:46.240
All right, Angela, question number four.

1102
00:51:46.640 --> 00:51:48.720
We got this fan question from Ella M.

1103
00:51:48.900 --> 00:51:50.200
in Louisiana who said,

1104
00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:54.040
Angela, your character changes so much over the course of nine seasons.

1105
00:51:54.400 --> 00:51:57.640
She shows emotion to Dwight and even Pam at times.

1106
00:51:57.940 --> 00:52:03.580
In early season, she's strictly private and severe, but toward the end, she is more open

1107
00:52:03.580 --> 00:52:06.120
with her coworkers and shows different sides of herself.

1108
00:52:06.420 --> 00:52:10.860
How did you show this new emotional side of Angela that people sympathize with in the

1109
00:52:10.860 --> 00:52:15.020
later seasons while also still maintaining her stern personality?

1110
00:52:15.340 --> 00:52:17.120
And also, Kristen A.

1111
00:52:17.320 --> 00:52:18.980
from Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania said,

1112
00:52:19.140 --> 00:52:24.280
looking back on Angela's journey, is there a particular moment where you feel she grew

1113
00:52:24.280 --> 00:52:28.180
the most as a character or one that you were particularly proud of her?

1114
00:52:28.180 --> 00:52:30.320
Such thoughtful questions.

1115
00:52:30.320 --> 00:52:35.780
I'm really just moved by the time and care that people put into writing these questions.

1116
00:52:36.100 --> 00:52:36.180
I know.

1117
00:52:36.300 --> 00:52:36.920
So good, right?

1118
00:52:36.920 --> 00:52:37.860
So nice of them.

1119
00:52:38.160 --> 00:52:42.420
One of the things I loved about the fact that our show was on for nine years

1120
00:52:42.420 --> 00:52:48.080
is that you really felt the passage of time with these characters, with this world.

1121
00:52:48.320 --> 00:52:52.620
And all of the characters got progressively more comfortable with the documentary crew being

1122
00:52:52.620 --> 00:52:52.920
there.

1123
00:52:53.060 --> 00:52:54.460
Just naturally, you would.

1124
00:52:55.000 --> 00:52:59.520
And I do think a lot of the characters over time evolved and changed.

1125
00:52:59.740 --> 00:53:05.780
And I always felt like for Angela, it was the love of Dwight that helped her come out of

1126
00:53:05.780 --> 00:53:06.220
her shell.

1127
00:53:07.260 --> 00:53:10.040
And he challenged her in so many ways.

1128
00:53:10.040 --> 00:53:11.660
And she loved the challenge.

1129
00:53:11.960 --> 00:53:16.620
It made her try new things and meet new people and do experiences that she would have

1130
00:53:16.620 --> 00:53:19.280
never done, except that she loved him.

1131
00:53:19.700 --> 00:53:22.200
And I think she was the better for it.

1132
00:53:22.200 --> 00:53:26.920
And Angela, do you have a moment where you were particularly proud of the character?

1133
00:53:27.460 --> 00:53:32.360
I was most proud of Angela's growth with her relationship to Oscar.

1134
00:53:32.700 --> 00:53:32.940
Yeah.

1135
00:53:33.140 --> 00:53:38.280
I always truly saw Oscar as Angela Martin's best friend in the office.

1136
00:53:38.820 --> 00:53:41.960
He's who she would talk to and commiserate with.

1137
00:53:41.960 --> 00:53:44.620
And they had the same tasted men.

1138
00:53:45.040 --> 00:53:45.400
Yes.

1139
00:53:45.780 --> 00:53:51.000
And although they certainly had their differences of opinion and they would often butt heads,

1140
00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:53.920
I always believe she really cared for him.

1141
00:53:54.180 --> 00:54:00.860
So I was really happy when the writers wrote the arc where Oscar offers Angela and baby Philip

1142
00:54:00.860 --> 00:54:02.360
to come live with him.

1143
00:54:02.540 --> 00:54:02.880
Mm-hmm.

1144
00:54:02.880 --> 00:54:06.160
Um, I loved their dynamic as roommates.

1145
00:54:06.560 --> 00:54:08.380
That was so much fun to play.

1146
00:54:08.940 --> 00:54:15.300
And if you watch the wedding of Dwight and Angela, it is Oscar who is taking care of baby

1147
00:54:15.300 --> 00:54:15.640
Philip.

1148
00:54:15.980 --> 00:54:16.360
Mm-hmm.

1149
00:54:16.820 --> 00:54:20.160
So she trusts him with the things that matter most to her.

1150
00:54:20.820 --> 00:54:22.240
Yeah, that's beautiful.

1151
00:54:22.600 --> 00:54:23.020
Yeah.

1152
00:54:23.020 --> 00:54:26.620
So that, that growth in that relationship meant a lot to me.

1153
00:54:26.880 --> 00:54:28.860
Well, listen, Angela, you also mentioned Dwight.

1154
00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:36.120
Lady, I know that you and Rain share a special bond after playing one another's fictional love

1155
00:54:36.120 --> 00:54:37.420
interests for nine years.

1156
00:54:37.820 --> 00:54:39.240
Can you talk a little bit about it?

1157
00:54:39.700 --> 00:54:39.940
Yeah.

1158
00:54:40.120 --> 00:54:46.080
I mean, you know, it's so interesting to me because pretty much right away, the first time

1159
00:54:46.080 --> 00:54:51.220
we had to be in a scene together where there was this undercurrent of romance, you know,

1160
00:54:51.220 --> 00:54:58.380
we just had an instant chemistry and he had sat at that desk across the way for a while.

1161
00:54:58.580 --> 00:54:59.760
You know, I don't know.

1162
00:54:59.920 --> 00:55:04.380
I didn't ever see who Angela's relationship would be in the office.

1163
00:55:04.640 --> 00:55:07.480
Like I just assumed she would have a relationship outside of the office.

1164
00:55:07.700 --> 00:55:12.720
And the minute the writers wrote that storyline, it just instantly made so much sense.

1165
00:55:12.900 --> 00:55:17.980
And I remember at the table read when it was revealed that their Birkenstocks were rubbing

1166
00:55:17.980 --> 00:55:23.660
back and forth at Jim's barbecue, the whole table, like the whole room let out this like,

1167
00:55:23.760 --> 00:55:24.840
oh, yeah.

1168
00:55:25.180 --> 00:55:26.640
And there was like a light bulb, light bulb.

1169
00:55:26.800 --> 00:55:30.680
Like everyone, it was like, oh my gosh, of course, of course, Dwight and Angela.

1170
00:55:31.080 --> 00:55:33.120
I hadn't thought of it at all, you know.

1171
00:55:33.360 --> 00:55:39.460
And then we didn't get much direction about our characters and who they were to each other

1172
00:55:39.460 --> 00:55:40.400
in this relationship.

1173
00:55:41.160 --> 00:55:47.160
We were given great dialogue, but all the rest of it just really happened spontaneously.

1174
00:55:47.740 --> 00:55:52.300
I think it was just Rain being Dwight and me being Angela Martin.

1175
00:55:53.040 --> 00:55:54.940
It just all kind of happened.

1176
00:55:54.940 --> 00:56:00.220
You know, you've said that thing where when you auditioned with John, it was like you guys

1177
00:56:00.220 --> 00:56:04.320
were doing this dance, but neither of you even knew it, you know?

1178
00:56:04.320 --> 00:56:10.620
It was as if they turned on a song and John and I both started dancing the same dance

1179
00:56:10.620 --> 00:56:12.540
routine, but we'd never talked about it.

1180
00:56:12.680 --> 00:56:15.040
Yeah, that's exactly what I felt with Rain.

1181
00:56:15.260 --> 00:56:23.660
I knew whatever he did as Dwight, it was so instinctively in me how Angela would respond.

1182
00:56:24.440 --> 00:56:27.020
And doing those scenes with him were such a joy.

1183
00:56:27.720 --> 00:56:33.080
And it's also funny, and I think maybe why we have chemistry, because we really are like

1184
00:56:33.080 --> 00:56:38.900
brother and sister, and we really just tease the crap out of each other, and we're real

1185
00:56:38.900 --> 00:56:40.320
smart asses to one another.

1186
00:56:41.860 --> 00:56:48.180
We just played doubles tennis together a while back, and he came up to me and he was like,

1187
00:56:48.320 --> 00:56:51.460
I think they're hitting them too because they know they'll get the point.

1188
00:56:51.600 --> 00:56:52.780
They're avoiding me.

1189
00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:55.060
And I said, Rain, they've hit them to you.

1190
00:56:55.140 --> 00:56:56.080
You couldn't run and get that.

1191
00:56:56.460 --> 00:57:00.360
Like you literally like do some cardio.

1192
00:57:00.760 --> 00:57:01.620
You know what I mean?

1193
00:57:01.620 --> 00:57:06.780
Like we just kind of always go at each other like that, but it's always loving.

1194
00:57:07.100 --> 00:57:11.580
And my favorite thing is when I get a text from him that's like, hey, idiot, I miss you.

1195
00:57:11.780 --> 00:57:13.540
And I'm like, I miss you too, dumbass.

1196
00:57:15.340 --> 00:57:16.820
But yeah, it was just there.

1197
00:57:17.020 --> 00:57:18.140
I don't know how to explain it.

1198
00:57:18.780 --> 00:57:19.820
All right, lady.

1199
00:57:20.320 --> 00:57:25.240
During our All About Pam episode, I shared about the music I listened to that made me cry

1200
00:57:25.240 --> 00:57:30.500
as Pam, and you said you would share your Angela Crying song, so do tell.

1201
00:57:30.780 --> 00:57:31.280
Okay.

1202
00:57:31.440 --> 00:57:36.200
Well, when I first started acting, I had taken a class that explored emotions and scenes.

1203
00:57:36.760 --> 00:57:41.280
You know, we would take a scene, we would write markers alongside the dialogue that would

1204
00:57:41.280 --> 00:57:45.220
show the emotion going on in the subtext of whatever we were saying.

1205
00:57:45.220 --> 00:57:50.960
And as part of that class, we explored how music can affect you and help you be prepared for a scene

1206
00:57:50.960 --> 00:57:52.020
that has a lot of emotion.

1207
00:57:52.420 --> 00:57:58.820
We had to pick a song, go up on stage, sit in a chair by ourselves facing the entire class.

1208
00:57:58.980 --> 00:58:04.260
The teacher would hit play, and then we would have to slowly cry throughout the song in front of

1209
00:58:04.260 --> 00:58:04.600
everyone.

1210
00:58:04.880 --> 00:58:06.020
Oh, my gosh.

1211
00:58:06.220 --> 00:58:09.100
It was so daunting and terrifying.

1212
00:58:09.700 --> 00:58:10.800
And I did it.

1213
00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:13.480
And this is the song I picked.

1214
00:58:13.500 --> 00:58:15.760
It's called That's the Way Love Goes.

1215
00:58:16.820 --> 00:58:25.160
I've been throwing horseshoes over my left shoulder.

1216
00:58:26.660 --> 00:58:34.020
I've spent most all my life searching for that force.

1217
00:58:34.260 --> 00:58:43.820
Holy clover, yet you ran with me, chasing my rainbow.

1218
00:58:46.980 --> 00:58:49.340
Honey, I love you too.

1219
00:58:50.600 --> 00:58:53.920
And that's the way love goes.

1220
00:58:56.240 --> 00:59:00.160
Okay, so you seem like you're getting a little choked up even just listening to that clip.

1221
00:59:00.380 --> 00:59:01.360
Does it still trigger you?

1222
00:59:01.640 --> 00:59:03.180
Yeah, I mean, okay.

1223
00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:07.920
When I picked that song, my dad was still alive, but my dad loved Merle Haggard.

1224
00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:08.600
Okay.

1225
00:59:08.600 --> 00:59:11.240
He loved all the old country musicians.

1226
00:59:11.540 --> 00:59:11.700
Yeah.

1227
00:59:11.960 --> 00:59:19.100
And the interesting thing for me now is that I think that song always just made me think of

1228
00:59:19.100 --> 00:59:26.440
just this unconditional love that I had from my family and what that means to me.

1229
00:59:27.320 --> 00:59:32.340
Now for me, when I have to get emotional on a scene, I don't need the song anymore.

1230
00:59:32.340 --> 00:59:37.360
I think a lot of life has happened to me, and I will sort of tap into some of the sadness

1231
00:59:37.360 --> 00:59:39.980
that life has brought, and then you're there.

1232
00:59:40.680 --> 00:59:46.660
And so I think hearing that song now and sharing it with you, because I've never shared it with

1233
00:59:46.660 --> 00:59:53.280
you before, and you're someone who holds such a special place in my heart that I just thought

1234
00:59:53.280 --> 00:59:59.560
about how thankful I am to have you in my life and to have this job that we get to do together.

1235
00:59:59.600 --> 01:00:06.660
So my tears and my emotion are very connected to the fact that I feel so safe sharing with you,

1236
01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:06.900
Jenna.

1237
01:00:07.100 --> 01:00:07.960
Oh my gosh.

1238
01:00:07.960 --> 01:00:13.800
So that's kind of why I got a little teary is that I was like, it felt so lovely to be able to share

1239
01:00:13.800 --> 01:00:15.540
something like that with you.

1240
01:00:15.960 --> 01:00:16.660
Well, thank you.

1241
01:00:16.880 --> 01:00:22.160
Lady, I'm so glad that we're doing this episode and that you're getting to share so much about

1242
01:00:22.160 --> 01:00:24.880
your process and your approach to character.

1243
01:00:24.880 --> 01:00:32.400
